{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וישלח אותם משה", :translit "vayishlach otam moshe", :gloss "And Moses sent them"} {:he "ס\"ת חמה", :translit "sofei tevot chamah", :gloss "final letters [spell] chamah"} {:he "חמה", :translit "chamah", :gloss "heat / wrath"} {:he "בכי טוב", :translit "b'khi tov", :gloss "in a good state"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וישלח אותם משה", :spells "חמה"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'enter with heat into the city' and the idiom 'a person enters b'khi tov and exits b'khi tov' appear in rabbinic literature. The underlying idea is that one should enter a city at a time of thriving so that one's departure will also be in a thriving state.", :sources ["Proverbs 14:28 (for the idiom of entering 'b'khi tov')"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ראשי בני ישראל המה", :translit "rashei b'nei yisra'el hemah", :gloss "Heads of the children of Israel, they are"} {:he "המה", :translit "hemah", :gloss "they are"} {:he "נ'", :translit "nun", :gloss "[the letter] nun / fifty"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "המה", :b "נ'"}], :frame {:text "The term 'sarim' (princes) is used for the tribal leaders sent as spies in Numbers 13:2–3. In biblical administration, officials over units of fifty are called 'sarei chamishim' (Exodus 18:21, Deuteronomy 1:15). Ba'al HaTurim links the gematria of 'hemah' to the number fifty to identify these leaders as officers over fifty.", :sources ["Exodus 18:21" "Deuteronomy 1:15" "Numbers 13:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וישלח אותם משה", :spells "חמה", :letters ["ח" "ם" "ה"], :by :selah, :order :in-order} {:kind :gematria, :a "המה", :a-value 50, :b "נ'", :b-value 50, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:28:41.330184994Z", :model "glm-5.2"}